<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matteo I Visconti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1250–1322</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matteo I Visconti was lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and the real founder of the Visconti state. Great-nephew of Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottone-visconti">Ottone Visconti</a>, he was named imperial vicar in 1294, was driven out by the della Torre in 1302 and returned with the support of Emperor Henry VII in 1311. A Ghibelline leader, he extended Milanese power over much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> but was excommunicated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xxii">Pope John XXII</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matteo-i-visconti.jpg" alt="Matteo I Visconti" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rijksmuseum · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portret_van_Matteo_Visconti,_RP-P-1874-12-11-1_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
